+# Mission 01 — CoopDX Metal Integration
+
+## Checkpoint (2026-11-25)
+
+- Independent project: `/home/larry/Projects/sm64coopdx-metal`
+- Branch: `mission-01-coopdx-foundation`
+- Upstream remote: `https://github.com/LeoManrique/sm64coopdx-ios.git`
+- Source baseline: `6bfcfede7a5c1af400daa269931a9ae13ed157ad` (upstream `main`)
+- Original Metal and Mission 82 worktrees were inspected and left untouched.
+- No renderer source has been changed in this checkout.
+
+## Baseline build
+
+| Validation | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Linux upstream build, `make -j2` | Passed; produced `build/us_pc/sm64coopdx` (x86-64 ELF) and generated game assets |
+| ROM input | Temporary local copy of the existing user ROM, ignored by Git; SHA-1 matched the repository's `sm64.us.sha1`; no ROM or generated game binary was staged |
+| `make test` | Blocked: the target invokes `mupen64plus`, which is not installed (`Error 127`) |
+| `./build_ios.sh generate` | Blocked before generation: `xcrun`/Xcode command line tools are unavailable in this Linux environment |
+| Linux rebuild after iOS import and ZIP hardening changes | Passed; `build/us_pc/sm64coopdx` linked |
+| ZIP path test | Passed with GCC and Clang ASan/UBSan |
+| Unsigned iOS app / IPA / iPhone run | Not produced or verified |
+
+The `build_ios.sh` flow requires macOS/Xcode, CMake and `xcrun`; its full `all` path also builds desktop assets before generating/building the Xcode iOS target and packaging the unsigned IPA. The checkout includes no iOS build workflow in `.github/workflows`; the existing workflow is for desktop builds.
+
+## Upstream architecture audit
+
+### Foundation and mod systems
+
+- The iOS project is the CoopDX source tree, rather than a separate Lua or DynOS implementation. It enables `DYNOS`, `COOP`, `COOPNET`, `HANDHELD`, and `TOUCH_CONTROLS`; ships its own Lua 5.3 iOS static library; and contains CoopDX's game, Lua, mod, DynOS, and network systems.
+- iOS uses SDL2 plus a UIKit bridge. The current iOS CMake configuration selects `RAPI_GL=1`, builds vendored SDL2 with iOS patches, and links OpenGLES, UIKit, GameController, CoreMotion, and audio frameworks. The default is therefore the upstream OpenGL ES backend.
+- `sys_user_path()` resolves to the app's Documents directory on iOS. CoopDX's normal filesystem/mod code uses this write root; mods and DynOS packs remain under the existing `mods` and `dynos` layouts and are rediscovered by the normal mod/DynOS managers.
+- `platform_ios.m` has a `UIDocumentPicker` path for importing a ROM. The Mods panel exposes the app's Mods folder in Files, and `gfx_sdl.c` routes SDL `DROPFILE` events through `mod_import_file()`. This branch adds an **Import Mod ZIP** action using `UIDocumentPicker`; the selected file is copied to a unique temporary path and queued through SDL so existing import and DynOS refresh logic runs on the game event thread.
+- The production ZIP importer detects Lua/DynOS content, then extracts into existing Mods or DynOS directories; normal refresh/load code handles activation and persistence. It is not a new package/runtime format. ZIP preflight rejects unsafe paths, more than 16,384 entries, entries above 128 MiB, or total uncompressed contents above 512 MiB. Extraction streams to disk instead of allocating whole files. The scan validates every archive member even after it finds a Lua script.
+- Added host path-validation tests with GCC and Clang ASan/UBSan. A production importer end-to-end ZIP fixture test and transactional rollback remain outstanding; extraction can leave partial files if a later member fails. UIKit and the complete import workflow are not Apple-compiled or device-tested because this environment has no Xcode.
+- Bundled example fixtures cover useful acceptance cases: `low-gravity.lua`, behavior replacement/addition and spawned objects, nested custom-animation examples for Lua and DynOS, custom HUD textures, texture overrides, custom levels, and a custom actor/collision model. These are candidate end-to-end checks; no runtime mod test has yet been claimed.
+- Import hardening is a prerequisite for treating arbitrary downloaded ZIPs as safe. Extraction still writes directly into installed folders and can leave partial files if a later entry fails; add an importer-level fixture test and consider rollback before treating untrusted ZIP installation as robust.
+
+### Renderer comparison and compatibility matrix
+
+The Metal reference is `LawrenceBanes/sm64ios-metal`, branch `mission-81-shader64-rendergraph`, commit `259bcaa19ab5c7cd39dab28f1080a650e5739a4c`. Its backend is an implementation in the existing Fast3D renderer, not an independent gameplay engine. CoopDX and that branch do **not** have a source-compatible rendering abstraction.
+
+| Component | Disposition | Reason / next action |
+|---|---|---|
+| CoopDX gameplay, Lua, mod/DynOS managers, multiplayer, SDL/UIKit lifecycle | Reuse directly as the game foundation | These are the compatibility priority and remain authoritative. |
+| CoopDX display-list interpreter (`gfx_pc.c`) and `GfxRenderingAPI` semantics | Preserve directly | The renderer continues to decode N64 display lists, texture state, geometry modes, and batching before invoking a backend. |
+| Existing Metal device/command-buffer/pipeline and shader-source work | Adapt selectively | Its public backend callbacks and shader key do not match CoopDX's callbacks or combiner semantics; port behind CoopDX's interface after licensing review and parity tests. |
+| Color combiner and shader pipeline | Reimplement/adapt semantically; do not cast/hash-only | CoopDX passes a `ColorCombiner*` to `lookup_shader()` and `create_and_load_new_shader()`. The combiner contains normalized combine-mode data, shader input mappings, shader commands, flags, and a cache hash. The Metal branch accepts `uint32_t shader_id` and separately decodes a packed ID into `CCFeatures`. Cache by a canonical semantic key and translate every supported combiner operation; a truncated value or hash-only equivalence is not correct. |
+| Vertex layout and dynamic geometry | Adapt | Preserve CoopDX's float VBO stream and draw batching. Map its actual attributes, camera/model transforms, and material inputs to Metal buffers without bypassing the interpreter. |
+| Texture conversion/upload/sampler state | Adapt | CoopDX performs N64 texture-format decoding and carries per-tile sampler modes. Retain that frontend contract; implement Metal texture caching/invalidation and wrap/filter state in the backend. |
+| Alpha/depth/blend/fog/decal/viewport/scissor | Adapt and verify per callback | Metal must match each state transition produced by CoopDX's renderer, including UI and DynOS draws. |
+| SDL2/UIKit and presentation | Adapt minimally | Keep upstream SDL2 window/lifecycle/input ownership; attach Metal to the UIKit/SDL view and honor its resize, pause/resume, and drawable lifecycle. |
+| Scene capture, SSAO, bloom, shadows, ray-tracing research | Defer | Only after vanilla Metal parity and mod compatibility. They must remain optional and must not affect the OpenGL ES fallback. |
+| Previous custom gameplay/world additions | Discard from this migration | Not needed for CoopDX's authoritative gameplay/mod base and explicitly out of scope. |
+
+### License and provenance
+
+The iOS repository is presented on GitHub as a fork of `coop-deluxe/sm64coopdx`, but the inspected root tree contains no LICENSE/COPYING file. An upstream repository issue also records that the project had no license and was marked Proprietary for a packaging metadata submission. Treat upstream code as the reference implementation until its reuse/distribution terms are resolved; do not copy backend code into this project based only on public visibility. Any eventual contribution strategy must preserve upstream history/attribution and be reviewed against actual rights.
+
+## Preserved working trees
+
+- `/home/larry/Projects/sm64ios-metal` remains on its pre-existing branch and has its pre-existing modified `src/game/skybox.c` untouched.
+- `/home/larry/Projects/sm64ios-mission82` remains on `mission-82-coopdx-native` at `854f63fd3c8e66a4f4107813551e239439696270`, including its prior uncommitted files untouched.
+- Mission 81 remains isolated; no changes from it were copied into the CoopDX checkout.
+
+## Next steps
+
+1. Add a production-importer fixture test using a synthetic ZIP, covering nested Lua modules, DynOS detection, traversal rejection and size bounds; improve rollback/partial-install behavior.
+2. Run bundled Lua and DynOS example mods through the upstream runtime and record observed behavior rather than inferring compatibility from fixture presence.
+3. Establish iOS arm64 compile/IPA automation on an authorized macOS runner using a private ROM/asset input path; verify the UIKit picker on device.
+4. Resolve upstream source reuse/distribution terms before copying backend code; then design the `ColorCombiner`→Metal semantic adapter and verify vanilla parity with OpenGL ES.
